I have Nick Gillespie with reason TV  today we're talking with David Stockman  a former congressman who is best  remembered as the budget director for  Ronald Reagan David thanks for talking  with us good to be here you're back in  the in the spotlight and getting roasted  again by your fellow Republicans because  you've been an outspoken advocate of  letting all of the Bush era tax rates  expire  what's your rationale behind that well  in pure philosophy lower tax rates would  be better the problem is we've had a  30-year referendum on spending every  aspect of the welfare state we've made a  tiny bit of progress in 81 that was  restored over the course of the next  couple decades then we finally had  Republican government in the Bush era  both in the Congress and in the White  House and nothing was cut everything was  ratified in fact they added to Medicare  through the drug benefit so now where's  the total federal outlays about 60  percent in 2010 dollars that's exactly  right so now we're at the point where we  have this large welfare state that seems  immovable politically and this expanding  warfare state that both parties seem to  want to fund and in that environment  you're kidding yourself if you think  cutting taxes today is really cutting  taxes we're simply deferring massive tax  increases into the future unfairly and  immorally putting huge debt burdens on  future generations and that is just  wrong there's a presumption there that  if we raised more federal revenue that  would go to pay what we're what we're  actually doing now as opposed to just  give the government more impetus to grow  even faster well that's always a risk  but I think the fact is there isn't much  pressure for structural expansion of the  budget right now does there need to be  though because I've been Medicare  spending set to continue to expand  particularly after 2020 I mean so there  doesn't need to be a politician saying  let's raise let's create whole new  entitlements because we've got  ones that are on autopilot to explode  right oh yes I mean the cost trajectory  of the entitlements and the program  structure that is there will rise over  time and part of the fiscal battle will  be to find ways to contain that so I  very strongly believe that in addition  to allowing the tax cuts to expire we  need to means-test  radically the retirement entitlements in  the past few years we've seen under Bush  and then continued under under President  Obama  things like tarp things like stimulus  spending I mean it started under Bush in  2008 was obviously kicked in to high  gear by Obama are we in a place now  where bailouts will become a constant  part of American life well that is a  pressure and that is a clear risk I  believe in some ways it was a profound  moment in political history in September  to O eight when Paulson panicked and of  course Bush was a deer in a headlight he  had no clue about what was going on and  stampeded the Republicans into enacting  tarp  I was tarp a bad idea I mean we have  President Bush we have President Obama  we have Tim Geithner we have a bunch of  Nobel prize-winning economists telling  us that we have Warren Buffett saying  tarp you know that's the only reason you  and I are still talking that's the only  reason why there's still lights on well  first of all that's Obon mythology I  don't think there was panic on Main  Street in America what was happening was  that the big pyramids of debt on Wall  Street were coming crashing down and had  we allowed nature to take its course  maybe the goldman sachs stock would have  gone down to ten dollars but that's  their problem and that's the problem of  speculators who own the stock not a  problem systemic problem for the economy  maybe a couple of banks would have been  closed by the FDIC and whether you  believe in Deposit Insurance or not  which I don't it was there and that was  the function of Deposit Insurance Oh  what what happened then why did that win  the political battle because Paulson  frankly is the most incompetent reckless  secretary of the  every we've had in modern history if  ever and he had no schooling in public  policy he had no schooling in the longer  term issues fiscal management or even  you know what sound money is all about  for crying out loud and as a result as  the crisis metastasized in September  October of 208 and he got all these  panic calls from his buddies on Wall  Street who were seeing their pyramids of  debt come crashing down and particularly  when the stock of goldman sachs sachs  started to plunge he panicked there was  no philosophy behind it there was never  an analysis done this whole idea that  there was systemic risk it's just a term  made up by people who were looking for  ways to meddle in the economy do you  think he ran with it should the feds  have the Fed or the feds or any portion  of the federal government should they  have bailed out Bear Stearns should they  have bailed out Lehman Brothers should  they bailed out goldman sachs should  they bailed at GM do do bailouts at any  point make sense no I think absolutely  not once the broad public sees that the  cronies of capitalism are bailed out by  their friends in Washington or the Fed  why should they believe that the system  we have is fair or is working in their  interest isn't this the oldest story of  American capitalism which is that there  is always at some point there is  somebody who's too big to fail or more  correctly to politically connected to  fail well I think the risk has always  been there but historically there was at  least in the Republican Party principled  beliefs that the marketplace had to be  allowed to operate and that both gains  and losses would occur and even though  there was pressure from time to time for  interventions and bailouts it was pretty  rare if at all during the Eisenhower  administration for instance even though  Ike was suspected of not being a good  consumer respected a bigger cop if you  look at the  Tomic policy of the Eisenhower era it  was pretty solid he actually believed in  balancing the budget and budget balanced  the budget three times during the Ford  era again President Ford was pretty much  a free enterprise believer and there  aren't too many examples that you could  come up with where there was outright  bailouts but I think the problem really  gathered gathered momentum in the late  70s after inflation got out of control  because we closed the gold window in  1971 then politicians began to find  reasons for expediency the Chrysler  bailout was a terrible thing even though  it kept the company alive it's the  company alive long enough so that it  could die again and take GM down with it  and cost the second Bay Road in the  Reagan era I thought we were free  enterprise and mostly we were but we  bailed out continental bank against the  strong belief of myself and many others  in the administration but it was kind of  forced through panic what things began  to what do you do with savings alone was  that systemic risk when the Savings and  Loan industry collapsed well first of  all the problem that blew up in the late  80s was encouraged by bad mistakes made  by the Reagan administration the early  eighties we deregulated the SNL's and  allowed them to speculate in all kinds  of real estate and land development and  so forth per se it's not a problem right  but it's problem if they have guaranteed  deposit insurance and if they have  federal entities to turn to to bail them  out if they make reckless mistakes well  all of them did and so we ended up with  insolvent institutions in mass in the  late 80s and I think they did more or  less a reasonable job of dealing with  that because all of them were closed and  liquidated and losses were absorbed by  the equity speculators  and the you know jr. bond speculators in  these failed SNL's the depositors were  protected because that's what the  deposit insurance system is but it was a  good lesson that we had danger built  into the heart of our banking system  because of Deposit Insurance which  allowed speculators to raise money  without people worrying about the risk  of investing and they had access to the  Fed window which says that if you really  go off the edge of the cliff and become  illiquid because you're investing long  and funding short which is foolish and  has always been frowned upon by sound  banking theory historically we should  have seen it coming  with in other words the S&amp;L crisis was a  wake-up call that Deposit Insurance and  the Fed were about ready to fuel a far  greater banking system bubble and blow  up and sure enough 15 years later that's  exactly what we had and then when the  crisis came we went in and bailed out  the whole thing again and have just  created the condition for even more  traumatic excess in the future now but  we've solved all our problems right with  the dodd-frank financial regulation bill  you know which is being touted certainly  by the Obama administration and I guess  by Chris Dodd and Barney Frank as you  know the be-all and end-all does that  change anything does it address any of  the issues that you're that you're  talking about and does it make you sleep  easier at night knowing that we're now  regulated in a different way well really  not at all because it's just another  sort of crony capitalist shuffle if we  really have a problem in our banking  system of too big to fail which i  profoundly believe we do then the  institutions involved are too big to  exist and by that I mean exist we have  access to the Federal Deposit Insurance  guarantees and therefore the ability to  raise hundreds of billions or trillions  of deposits  and with access to the Fed window so  what happened at the end of 207 before  the crisis actually hit fully the top  four banks in this country had five  trillion of asset footings combined  after the whole crisis of too big to  fail and all of the bailouts and putting  the taxpayer in harm's way in a manner  never done before today the top four  banks have seven trillion of asset  footings so what we did and what the  FIDIC and the Federal Reserve did in  their wisdom was to actually just  steeping and enlarge the problem so I  believe in narrow banking I believe that  if you're going to have Deposit  Insurance it should be for banks that  can lend for only short periods of time  for commercial activities where the risk  of loss is minimal and if you want to be  in the speculation business if you want  to be in the over-the-counter  derivatives marketing business if you  want to make long-term loans and  speculative real estate and even  business enterprises fine  be my guest do it as a free enterprise  financial institution but don't look for  guaranteed deposits to raise your  funding and don't think you should act  have access to the Fed window in case  you make a discount window that is in  the event that you make large mistakes  that is the fundamental line of  demarcation that needs to be addressed  and the legislation passed by Congress  the so-called thin reg doesn't do any of  that it simply involves 2300 pages of an  abling Act which empowers the same  institutions who fostered this crisis  the Fed the FDIC the controller to  determine what those mean and it more or  less is a full-employment Act for  lobbyists lawyers accounting consultants  and so forth to find ways to  build loopholes which is easy enough to  do now in the New York Times over the  summer you laid one of the four  deformations of the fiscal apocalypse on  the heads of pointless speculation and  stocks bonds commodities and derivatives  are you arguing that essentially that  these this trade only existed because of  regulations or of a government system or  a political economy where people knew if  they were playing around with that kind  of stuff that they would get bailed out  well I think it goes beyond or further  back than that I think it goes to the  closing of the gold window in 1971 it  goes to the and that because the last  shred of anchoring the US dollar to a  gold standard yes and because that  system as it had its defects Bretton  Woods but at the heart of it was a fixed  exchange rate system and an obligation  on each company in country including the  United States to settle its accounts at  the end of every year and if you began  to run chronic payments deficits you  were going to under that system lose  your monetary reserves goal of the fewer  the United States or dollar reserve  assets if you were other participants in  the system and what that did was foster  continuous financial discipline because  any country losing large-scale reserves  would find its banking system  contracting the interest rates rising  its economy slowing down and the basis  for sustainable adjustment to occur now  when we got rid of all of that and went  to pure fiat money it began a process in  which financial volatility and  instability reached what had been  previously unimaginable levels and so if  you have exchange rates careening all  over the place if you have interest  rates soaring and then declining and  then bouncing to and fro the market will  invent  Hedges which is to say currency futures  and hedges of every kind interest rate  hedges of every imaginable kind in order  to meet the need of participants in the  system to have some certainty about the  value value of things so I think much of  this massive you know 700 trillion  derivatives market which is out there  was fostered by the breakdown of fixed  exchange rates in the anchor of the  monetary system to a asset gold in that  case that couldn't be manipulated for  short-term purposes by governments in  central bank's once these things were  invented because of the turbulence in  the market for legitimate hedging there  was nothing to stop all the casino  players from jumping in and simply  speculating you know this is probably a  good time 