he warfare  state right and of the military and of a  adventurist  were you always a Republican no I was a  SDS quasi Marxist radical then as I went  to Capitol Hill as a staffer I began to  see the virtues of what I would call the  libertarian view which isn't that far  from in some ways they're the radical  left and we looked at it that I felt  what's the common thread well I think  the common thread is that you know  individual action and  freedom and liberty to pursue both  economic or other ends is the core of  what society should be built on and so I  became very interested in free-market  economics as a staffer on Capitol Hill  that I was elected to Congress pursued  that talk a little bit about who were  you a staffer for and then how you know  to say and then I was elected to  Congress as if you know you were walking  down the street and got hit by something  falling out of the window what what went  into those determinations well I was a  staffer for Congressman John Anderson  who was chairing the Republican caucus  considered a moderate but actually he  was the right kind of Republican in my  view because he was a social liberal and  an economic conservative and the work I  did was a lot of things on deregulating  transportation let's say the minimum  wage being counterproductive the need to  reorganize or eliminate the whole Great  Society set of programs that had  developed and so forth on the other hand  he as a moderate on social issues didn't  believe the government ought to be  intervening in our private lives and I  agree with that I agree with that then  and I still do now so it was out of that  period that I more fully form my  philosophy I ran for Congress then and  was elected in 1976 and had a chance to  begin to develop those ideas  legislatively maybe culminating in two  battles that I fought three really  before I went into the Reagan White  House one was deregulation natural gas  but that was the leading edge of the  whole fight with Cartwright statist  energy policy I was on the Energy  Committee and was one of the leaders of  the fight for deregulated natural gas  but opposing all the other Carter  nonsense like syn fuels cooperation and  regulatory standards for the energy  efficiency of toasters and so forth so I  began to see there the danger of  industrial policy or economic policy and  had some good experience fighting it and  it essentially or to that I mean Carter  was really very good at deregulating  because he also deregulated interstate  trucking railroads and airplanes but  then was just a statist in other in kind  of fanciful dreams that he had right  yeah well I think he depended on the  advisors he had some advisors who could  see the insanity of the CA B Civil  Aeronautics Board or the ICC trying to  set tens of thousands of rail tariffs  and so forth trucking tariffs but then  in the energy side because again of  short-run panic about what was going on  with OPEC and the oil price which really  was a function of inflationary monetary  policy but the bad monetary policy led  to a perception that there was something  wrong in the energy economy which  generated you know professor Schlesinger  and all his minions running around  trying to create a state-run energy  sector so I fought that and I learned a  lot about the danger then shortly after  that came the Chrysler bailout room and  I was from Michigan there were a lot of  Chrysler's suppliers in my district but  I strongly support opposed it gave a  loud speech on the floor about it found  that Lee Iacocca was about ready to run  me out of the state on a rail or wished  he could have brought but you know will  he have used a Chrysler but the thing is  I was right not only that but in the  next election I got the largest vote  ever so see what what does that say  about political economy though because I  mean there you are in Michigan and  you're running against one of the  fair-haired boys one of the major  employers in the state 