m all  right the top tax rate on earned income  was 50% of the time and I think we ended  up in the low 40s but it was about a 30  percent cut the the thing I learned out  of that tax cutting exercise was twofold  one is that and this is still not  focused on  there wasn't much popular support for  across-the-board rate reductions that  would benefit the average citizen or  even entrepreneur businessman the only  way that we got that through despite all  the urban legend that exists today was  through a massive Christmas tree of  special interest tax benefits and  loopholes that were appended to it the  numbers are simple the revenue reduction  was a trillion dollars over five years  from the tax cut half of it five hundred  billion was for the rate reduction the  other half was for oil depletion  incentives real estate tax breaks so in  other words you're giving back half of  that by well through three good yes and  so we ended up reducing the revenue far  beyond what was feasible even if there  had been a determination to really cut  spending but what happened subsequently  was almost no spending was cut right and  the defense budget soared out of control  we knew going in that there was a  commitment by President Reagan to  rebuild defense capability but what  happened in the early days was that the  Pentagon distorted campaign promises for  real growth of five percent which they  then raised a seven percent and Reagan  went along and then they started it from  a much higher base which is to say a  supplemental bill and as a result of  that defense spending got totally out of  control even as even though  discretionary non-defense discretionary  spending was was coming down a little  bit it did I doubt all that model we  caught very little out of the  entitlements other other than some  things that were appropriate and have  survived food stamp reform and AFDC but  the middle class entitlements weren't  cut there the whole complex of veterans  programs weren't cu